First bring-up of the rear camera: power up the IMX682 and see who answers

WHAT HAPPENS
------------
The surya has no driver for any of its five sensors, so libcamera literally
says "No sensor found for /dev/media0" and KDE's camera application answers
that there is no camera. And it is true: there is none.

The capture engine, on the other hand, DOES work. It is proven by moving real
pixels with the CSID's internal pattern generator, without any sensor:
csid0 -> vfe0_rdi0 -> /dev/video0, 1920x1080 in 10-bit Bayer, full range. So
the only thing missing between the device and a photo is the sensor.

WHAT WAS ALREADY TRIED WITHOUT TOUCHING THE KERNEL, AND WHY IT IS NOT ENOUGH
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
The five sensors hang off the WL2866D, a four-LDO chip on i2c-9 @0x28 with no
driver in Linux. Reading it revealed why no I2C scan ever found a sensor: when
Linux boots, its power-on register reads 0x00, meaning the four rails are OFF.

From user space it got this far:

  1. turn the four rails on over I2C          -> works
  2. release the reset with gpioset on the TLMM -> works
  3. scan the bus                             -> nobody shows up

The MCLK is missing. Sony sensors do not wake their I2C block until they have
an input clock, and that clock comes out of the camcc, which cannot be turned
on from user space. That is exactly the gap this patch fills, and that is why
the module is so short.

WHERE THE WIRING COMES FROM
---------------------------
It is not guessed. It comes from the surya's own Android kernel
(sdmmagpie-camera-sensor-idp.dtsi), and it is confirmed a second time by the
pin names this very file already declares:

    CAM_MCLK1  = GPIO_14      MCLK1 at 19.2 MHz
    CAM1_RST_N = GPIO_25      rear camera reset
    HAPTIC_INT_N = GPIO_26    <- the anchor: already used in this file

That pin 26 matches the haptic already written here is what proves the list's
numbering is not off by one.

Careful with the neighbouring file in the factory tree (-qrd.dtsi): it
describes Qualcomm's reference board and powers things from a pm8009 PMIC that
this phone does not carry. The right one is recognised because it uses
wide_ldo_avdd2 and camera_vdig, which are the regulators this device tree
already declares.

And the address: the comment that was here said "@ 50". Measured, the 0x50 on
that bus is the module's calibration EEPROM -it answers even with the camera
unpowered-, not the sensor. The sensor is at 0x10, which is where the GPL
drivers for the same chip for MediaTek put it.

SECOND ROUND: the regulator, so that it need not be turned on by hand
---------------------------------------------------------------------
With the sensor confirmed to answer, one loose end remained: its rails had to
be turned on by hand over I2C before binding the driver, so at boot the camera
was still dead.

The WL2866D driver is added (drivers/regulator/wl2866d.c), the four-LDO chip
that powers the five sensors. There is no public datasheet: the register map
comes from the surya's own factory driver and it is verified BY READING THE
CHIP -its power-on values, 0x64 0x64 0x80 0x80, match the formulas to the bit-.
That makes it a reading, not an assumption.

The four rails are declared always-on, and it is a conscious shortcut: it is
not yet known which one powers each of the five sensors. When the split is
known, each sensor will request its own and this comes out.

The chip's enable pin is deliberately NOT declared in the device tree. The
WL2866D already answers today at 0x28 without anyone touching it, so that pin
is already high; and the mainline device tree names that same PMIC pin as
something else. When in doubt, do not drive a pin whose function is not clear.

THIRD ROUND: a real driver, with an image as the goal
------------------------------------------------------
It is no longer a detector: the module registers a v4l2_subdev, is wired to
csiphy0 and carries the sensor's register tables. A single mode, 8000x6000 in
RAW10 at about 5 fps, without autofocus or HDR.

Where the tables come from: from MediaTek's GPL driver for THE SAME sensor and
THE SAME module vendor (imx682sunny), which is one of the two this phone
carries. The surya's factory file is NO GOOD for this, and not for lack of
looking at it: it has 387 register writes in total, and an IMX682
initialisation is over 600.

And the decision that removes the most risk: **the MCLK goes up to 24 MHz**.
The source tables are calculated for 24, the factory Android uses 19.2, and
adapting them would force recalculating the sensor's PLL multipliers -which is
precisely where the error creeps in that gives you a sensor which starts up and
emits nothing-. The SM7150's camera clock supports both frequencies
(camcc-sm7150.c), so there is no need to touch a single table. It goes in two
places that have to agree: the device tree and the driver.

The link figures come from the PLL of the tables themselves, not from a
catalogue: INCK 24 MHz / IOP_PREPLLCK_DIV 4 * IOP_PLL_MPY 416 = 2496 MHz, and
in D-PHY the signal is double edge, so that is 1248 MHz of link frequency and
2.5 Gbps per lane. That is the maximum of D-PHY 1.2: the manufacturer uses all
of it.

FIRST ATTEMPT AT TRANSMITTING, AND WHAT IT TAUGHT
-------------------------------------------------
With the manufacturer's tables as they are, CAMSS answered:

    qcom-camss ace0000.camss: Pixel clock is too high for CSIPHY
    qcom-camss ace0000.camss: Failed to power up pipeline: -22

It is not a sensor fault nor a wiring fault: the whole pipeline validated and
the only problem was the speed. The manufacturer configures the link at
1248 MHz, that is 2.5 Gbps per lane -the maximum of D-PHY 1.2, used in full-,
and the SM7150's CSIPHY does not reach it: its timer clock stops at 300 MHz
(csiphy_res_7150 in camss.c) and the driver demands link_freq/4 plus margin
below that.

The two sensor PLL multipliers are lowered, from 360/416 to 294/340, which
leaves the link at 1020 MHz. BOTH of them and in the same proportion: lowering
only the output one would leave the sensor generating pixels faster than it can
get them out, and that does not give an error, it gives a corrupt image.

The price is about 4.2 frames per second at 48 megapixels. For a first image
that is plenty.

And a piece of good news hidden in that error: to get as far as complaining
about the clock, CAMSS had already read the sensor's link frequency, so the
controls are correctly set and so is the device tree's link.

THE RISK THAT REMAINS, SAID PLAINLY
-----------------------------------
D-PHY with 2 lanes is configured because CAMSS only supports D-PHY, and because
2 lanes works whether the board carries 2 or 4. What cannot be known by reading
is whether this phone's camera is physically on C-PHY: if it is, there will be
no image and the rear camera will be a dead end with CAMSS.

It will show up as VFE sof timeout and no frames. Warning: CSIPHY lane errors
are SILENT in mainline, there is no message to look for.

From here on the sensor declares its endpoint towards csiphy0. That has a price
worth knowing: if the driver failed to register its subdev, CAMSS's async
notifier would not complete and /dev/media0 would be lost. That is why it had
not been declared until now.

WHAT THE MODULE DOES
--------------------
Deliberately little: it powers up in the order Sony asks for (voltages, then
clock, and the reset last), scans the bus, reads the identification and powers
back down. It does NOT register any V4L2 subdevice yet.

That it does not register a subdevice is on purpose and is not laziness: if an
endpoint towards csiphy0 were declared and the sensor failed to register,
camss's async notifier would never complete and we would lose /dev/media0 -and
with it the pattern capture, which today is the only good reference there is-.
First it is confirmed that the sensor wakes up; the wiring to camss comes
afterwards.

It scans the whole bus instead of reading only its address because the 0x10
comes from another tree, not from a measurement on this device. If only 0x10
were read, a "does not answer" would not distinguish between a wrong address,
the clock, the power or the reset. By scanning, any new address relative to the
powered-down bus already proves that the power-up works.

WHAT WAS MEASURED ON THE FIRST ROUND
------------------------------------
With the WL2866D's rails turned on by hand, the module loaded and the clock
running, a NEW address appeared on the bus that was not there with the camera
powered down:

    imx682: powered up: clock at 19200000 Hz, reset released
    imx682:   answers 0x1a          <-- new
    imx682:   answers 0x50          <-- the EEPROM, always shows up

So the power-up sequence WORKS and the 0x10 that the MediaTek drivers give is
not valid on this phone. That is why the device tree now points at 0x1a, which
is the measured address.

And that is why the scan asks EVERY address that answers for its
identification, instead of assuming: on this bus the module's EEPROM, the focus
motor and the sensor live together, and all three answer. Only one will say
0x0682.

WHAT WAS MEASURED ON THE FIRST ROUND
------------------------------------
With the WL2866D's rails turned on by hand, the module loaded and the clock
running, a NEW address appeared on the bus that was not there with the camera
powered down:

    imx682: powered up: clock at 19200000 Hz, reset released
    imx682:   answers 0x1a          <-- new
    imx682:   answers 0x50          <-- the EEPROM, always shows up

So the power-up sequence WORKS, and the 0x10 that the MediaTek drivers give for
this same chip is not valid on this phone. The device tree now points at 0x1a,
which is the measured address and not the inherited one.

And that is why the scan now asks EVERY address that answers for its
identification, instead of assuming which one is the sensor: on this bus the
module's EEPROM, the focus motor and the sensor live together, and all three
answer the scan. Only one will say 0x0682.

WHAT TO EXPECT IN THE LOG
-------------------------
    imx682 12-001a: powered up: clock at 19200000 Hz, reset released
    imx682 12-001a: scanning Qualcomm-CCI...
    imx682 12-001a:   answers 0x1a, identification 0x0682   <== IT IS THE IMX682
    imx682 12-001a:   answers 0x50, no readable identification
    imx682 12-001a: IT IS AN IMX682: identification 0x0682

If 0x1a turned out to be the focus motor and not the sensor, it will show up
right here: it will say a different identification, and the right one will be
at another of the addresses the scan enumerates.

If only 0x50 shows up, the sensor is still asleep and the next suspect is the
rail that goes through the WL2866D, which this patch does not yet control (it
has to be turned on by hand first with surya/camara/encender-camaras).

